# Chapter 3: Positing

Positing gives predication positions standing. The standing is not Boolean truth.
It is bilattice-valued: evidence for and evidence against are tracked independently.

This is where the familiar values true and false become only two corners of a
larger four-corner value object. The other corners are no information and
overdetermined information. This is the smallest setting where contradiction can be
held without making the system explode.

## Value as independent evidence

The basic value reading is not "true or false." It is "how much evidence for, and
how much evidence against?" In the strict four-valued case this gives the familiar
shape:

* no evidence;
* evidence for;
* evidence against;
* evidence both for and against.

The point is not to celebrate contradiction. The point is to make contradiction a
lawful state rather than an immediate collapse of the whole system.

## Two orders, not one

The same value object carries two readings. The truth order asks whether the value
is more true. The knowledge order asks whether the value contains more evidence.
Those are different questions, and Obsmetrologia keeps them different.

This is where many later recoveries begin. Classical truth appears as a collapse of
the richer value structure. Paraconsistent equality, truth-order equality,
hyperdoctrine adjoints, and the bigraded diamond all rely on refusing that collapse
too early.

## The closure this chapter asks for

The reader must cross from position to standing without collapsing standing into
Boolean truth. The operative icon is the four-valued bilattice: not because four
is decorative, but because independent support and opposition generate four
possible evidence states.

The repair locator for this chapter is the two-order distinction. If a later
argument cannot say whether it is moving by truth order or knowledge order, the
chapter has lost its transport discipline.
